## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of encryption, key management, cross-account access, and operational rotation at scale with compliance demands.

## Key Concepts

- Envelope encryption
- KMS CMKs and data keys
- Key rotation strategies
- Background re-encryption of existing data
- IAM/bucket policies per tenant
- Auditability with CloudTrail

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: rotate tenant data keys
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const kms = new AWS.KMS();
async function rotateTenantDataKey(cmkArn) {
  const { CiphertextBlob, Plaintext } = await kms.generateDataKey({ KeyId: cmkArn, KeySpec: 'AES_256' }).promise();
  // Store CiphertextBlob in tenant metadata; use Plaintext to encrypt new data in memory (do not persist plaintext)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test rotation in CI/CD without impacting production?
- How do you handle key revocation and tenant offboarding while preserving audits?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests proficiency designing fine-grained, scalable access controls for multi-tenant data lakes using ABAC, tags, and policy syntax.

## Key Concepts
- IAM tags and aws:PrincipalTag
- Resource policies with aws:RequestTag and stringEquals conditions
- S3 bucket prefixes per tenant and least privilege
- Testing cross-tenant access and tag revocation

## Code Example
```json
// Trust policy (IAM role)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT:role/TenantAccess"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// Permission policy
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:RequestTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// Bucket policy (alternative strict guard)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT:role/TenantAccess"},
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"s3:ExistingObjectTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate tags and ensure no stale access remains?
- How would you integrate with CI to validate policies in a sandbox before prod?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical serverless wiring, least-privilege IAM, and cross-account access basics.

## Key Concepts
- Lambda IAM roles, API Gateway integration, S3 PutObject, SSE-KMS, cross-account roles.
- KMS key policies and bucket policies.
- End-to-end testing of write and cross-account read.

## Code Example
```javascript
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const s3 = new AWS.S3();
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const payload = JSON.stringify(event);
  await s3.putObject({
    Bucket: process.env.LOG_BUCKET,
    Key: `logs/${Date.now()}.json`,
    Body: payload,
    ServerSideEncryption: 'aws:kms'
  }).promise();
  return { statusCode: 200, body: 'ok' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test failure modes (kms failure, bucket policy denial)?
- How would you rotate the KMS key and propagate changes?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross-account data governance at scale beyond single-service configs.

## Key Concepts

- Lake Formation cross-account data sharing
- IAM roles with trusted principals and external IDs
- RAM for resource sharing
- KMS cross-account encryption
- EventBridge/Glue orchestration
- Auditing and revocation testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: assume cross-account role and write a marker to shared S3
const { STSClient, AssumeRoleCommand } = require("@aws-sdk/client-sts");
const { S3Client, PutObjectCommand } = require("@aws-sdk/client-s3");

async function testAccess(roleArn, bucket, key, body) {
  const sts = new STSClient({});
  const r = await sts.send(new AssumeRoleCommand({ RoleArn: roleArn, RoleSessionName: "AuditTest" }));
  const s3 = new S3Client({ credentials: {
    accessKeyId: r.Credentials.AccessKeyId,
    secretAccessKey: r.Credentials.SecretAccessKey,
    sessionToken: r.Credentials.SessionToken
  }});
  await s3.send(new PutObjectCommand({ Bucket: bucket, Key: key, Body: body }));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test and enforce revocation across all accounts?
- How would you verify no data exposure in an incident scenario across accounts?

